.TH "PYOTP" "1" "Jun 16, 2026" "" "PyOTP"
.SH "نام (NAME)"
pyotp \- PyOTP 
.sp
PyOTP is a Python library for generating and verifying one\-time passwords. It can be used to implement two\-factor (2FA)
or multi\-factor (MFA) authentication methods in web applications and in other systems that require users to log in.
.sp
Open MFA standards are defined in RFC 4226 \%<https://\:tools\:.ietf\:.org/\:html/\:rfc4226> (HOTP: An HMAC\-Based One\-Time
Password Algorithm) and in RFC 6238 \%<https://\:tools\:.ietf\:.org/\:html/\:rfc6238> (TOTP: Time\-Based One\-Time Password
Algorithm). PyOTP implements server\-side support for both of these standards. Client\-side support can be enabled by
sending authentication codes to users over SMS or email (HOTP) or, for TOTP, by instructing users to use Google
Authenticator \%<https://\:en\:.wikipedia\:.org/\:wiki/\:Google_Authenticator>, Authy \%<https://\:www\:.authy\:.com>, or another
compatible app. Users can set up auth tokens in their apps easily by using their phone camera to scan otpauth:// \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:google/\:google-authenticator/\:wiki/\:Key-Uri-Format> QR codes provided by PyOTP.
.sp
Implementers should read and follow the HOTP security requirements \%<https://\:tools\:.ietf\:.org/\:html/\:rfc4226#\:section-7>
and TOTP security considerations \%<https://\:tools\:.ietf\:.org/\:html/\:rfc6238#\:section-5> sections of the relevant RFCs. At
minimum, application implementers should follow this checklist:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Ensure transport confidentiality by using HTTPS
.IP \(bu 2
Ensure HOTP/TOTP secret confidentiality by storing secrets in a controlled access database
.IP \(bu 2
Deny replay attacks by rejecting one\-time passwords that have been used by the client (this requires storing the most
recently authenticated timestamp, OTP, or hash of the OTP in your database, and rejecting the OTP when a match is
seen)
.IP \(bu 2
Throttle (rate limit) brute\-force attacks against your application\(aqs login functionality (see RFC 4226, section 7.3)
.IP \(bu 2
When implementing a \(dqgreenfield\(dq application, consider supporting
FIDO U2F \%<https://\:en\:.wikipedia\:.org/\:wiki/\:Universal_2nd_Factor>/WebAuthn \%<https://\:www\:.w3\:.org/\:TR/\:webauthn> in
addition to or instead of HOTP/TOTP. U2F uses asymmetric cryptography to avoid using a shared secret design, which
strengthens your MFA solution against server\-side attacks. Hardware U2F also sequesters the client secret in a
dedicated single\-purpose device, which strengthens your clients against client\-side attacks. And by automating scoping
of credentials to relying party IDs (application origin/domain names), U2F adds protection against phishing attacks.
One implementation of FIDO U2F/WebAuthn is PyOTP\(aqs sister project, PyWARP \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:pyauth/\:pywarp>\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
We also recommend that implementers read the
OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:OWASP/\:CheatSheetSeries/\:blob/\:master/\:cheatsheets/\:Authentication_Cheat_Sheet\:.md> and
NIST SP 800\-63\-3: Digital Authentication Guideline \%<https://\:pages\:.nist\:.gov/\:800-63-3> for a high level overview of
authentication best practices.
.SH QUICK OVERVIEW OF USING ONE TIME PASSWORDS ON YOUR PHONE
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
OTPs involve a shared secret, stored both on the phone and the server
.IP \(bu 2
OTPs can be generated on a phone without internet connectivity
.IP \(bu 2
OTPs should always be used as a second factor of authentication (if your phone is lost, you account is still secured
with a password)
.IP \(bu 2
Google Authenticator and other OTP client apps allow you to store multiple OTP secrets and provision those using a QR
Code
.UNINDENT
.SH INSTALLATION
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pip install pyotp
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH "نحوه استفاده (USAGE)"
.SS Time\-based OTPs
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import pyotp
import time

totp = pyotp.TOTP(\(aqbase32secret3232\(aq)
totp.now() # => \(aq492039\(aq

# OTP verified for current time
totp.verify(\(aq492039\(aq) # => True
time.sleep(30)
totp.verify(\(aq492039\(aq) # => False
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Counter\-based OTPs
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import pyotp

hotp = pyotp.HOTP(\(aqbase32secret3232\(aq)
hotp.at(0) # => \(aq260182\(aq
hotp.at(1) # => \(aq055283\(aq
hotp.at(1401) # => \(aq316439\(aq

# OTP verified with a counter
hotp.verify(\(aq316439\(aq, 1401) # => True
hotp.verify(\(aq316439\(aq, 1402) # => False
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Generating a Secret Key
.sp
A helper function is provided to generate a 32\-character base32 secret, compatible with Google Authenticator and other
OTP apps:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pyotp.random_base32()
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Some applications want the secret key to be formatted as a hex\-encoded string:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pyotp.random_hex()  # returns a 40\-character hex\-encoded secret
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Google Authenticator Compatible
.sp
PyOTP works with the Google Authenticator iPhone and Android app, as well as other OTP apps like Authy. PyOTP includes
the ability to generate provisioning URIs for use with the QR Code scanner built into these MFA client apps:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pyotp.totp.TOTP(\(aqJBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP\(aq).provisioning_uri(name=\(aqalice@google.com\(aq, issuer_name=\(aqSecure App\(aq)

>>> \(aqotpauth://totp/Secure%20App:alice%40google.com?secret=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP&issuer=Secure%20App\(aq

pyotp.hotp.HOTP(\(aqJBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP\(aq).provisioning_uri(name=\(dqalice@google.com\(dq, issuer_name=\(dqSecure App\(dq, initial_count=0)

>>> \(aqotpauth://hotp/Secure%20App:alice%40google.com?secret=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP&issuer=Secure%20App&counter=0\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This URL can then be rendered as a QR Code (for example, using \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:soldair/\:node-qrcode>) which can then be
scanned and added to the users list of OTP credentials.
.sp
Parsing these URLs is also supported:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
pyotp.parse_uri(\(aqotpauth://totp/Secure%20App:alice%40google.com?secret=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP&issuer=Secure%20App\(aq)

>>> <pyotp.totp.TOTP object at 0xFFFFFFFF>

pyotp.parse_uri(\(aqotpauth://hotp/Secure%20App:alice%40google.com?secret=JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP&issuer=Secure%20App&counter=0\(aq

>>> <pyotp.totp.HOTP object at 0xFFFFFFFF>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Working example
.sp
Scan the following barcode with your phone\(aqs OTP app (e.g. Google Authenticator):
[image]
.sp
Now run the following and compare the output:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
import pyotp
totp = pyotp.TOTP(\(dqJBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP\(dq)
print(\(dqCurrent OTP:\(dq, totp.now())
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Third\-party contributions
.sp
The following third\-party contributions are not described by a standard, not officially supported, and provided for
reference only:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpyotp.contrib.Steam()\fP: An implementation of Steam TOTP. Uses the same API as \fIpyotp.TOTP()\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
